+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line (blank lines are ignored).
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line. This makes the output easy to consume in shell loops:
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1 | while read v; do echo "$v"; done
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If any version is invalid, nothing is printed on standard out and the
+return code is ``2``.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to consider. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line (blank lines are ignored).
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1 1.10.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ printf '1.2.0\n1.3.0\n' | pysemver latest
+    1.3.0
+
+If any version is invalid, or none of the versions is a non-prerelease,
+nothing is printed on standard out and the return code is ``2``.
+
+
 Return Code
